 \noindent\textbf{Abstract.}
-This paper begins from a broader epistemological question: can a representational system fully perceive or justify the boundary that generates its own capacity to represent? The question is not answered here in full. Instead, the paper isolates and proves a bounded mathematical result beneath it. A source space \(X\) is mapped to an observer-accessible space \(O\) by \(\pi:X\to O\), and an admissible experiment receives only the resulting observation. Equality of observations induces fibers and an observable quotient. Quantities computable from the accessible observation are constant on those fibers, and a globally exact decoder can exist only when \(\pi\) is injective. The analysis is extended to interface refinement, generative models, observation-only experiments, empirical model quotients, and strict latent covers. A hidden-coordinate construction preserves reachable observations and admissible outcomes while increasing latent multiplicity. The synthesis theorem therefore establishes that empirical completeness at a declared interface does not, by itself, establish latent uniqueness or exact reconstruction. Finite simulations evaluate hidden multiplicity, observation fibers, admissible experiments, predicate profiles, empirical classes, and interface refinement in explicitly enumerated models. The broader program is then stated explicitly but not conflated with the theorem: paradox as a possible signal of representational closure, embedded self-modeling, scale-conditioned representability, competing metaphysical models, constraint-based accounts of unified states, and proposed studies of death, electromagnetic emission, and external-source hypotheses. These are classified as interpretations, hypotheses, or future work and are subject to a strict rule: no ontological hypothesis earns support without a discriminating prediction that empirically equivalent alternatives do not share.
+An observation map can preserve every recorded output while failing to determine the latent state that produced it. We study this distinction for a deterministic interface \(\pi:X\to O\). Exact decoding requires a left inverse and therefore injectivity; quantities determined by the observation are constant on its fibers. At the model level, we define empirical equivalence as equality of reachable observation sets. For every model with a nonempty latent space, adjoining an unobserved Boolean coordinate gives an equivalent model without an exact decoder. If the original is exactly decodable, the two models have opposite decoder statuses. Interface refinement describes which additional distinctions a measurement can retain. Exhaustive finite calculations reproduce the hidden multiplicities, collision counts, and refinement maps, and agree with twelve archived output tables. The result concerns set based identifiability within the declared model class. It does not establish equality of probability laws, impossibility of reconstruction from temporal measurements, or physical existence of the added coordinate. The proofs and computational details are supplied in appendices and a companion supplement.
 
-\vspace{0.5em}
-\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} observer interface; embedded observer; empirical equivalence; latent nonidentifiability; exact reconstruction; quotient space; interface refinement; self-reference; formal methods.
