-
-The distinction is essential. Direct experience can motivate a formal question. A theorem can establish a structural limit. Neither step, by itself, proves a preferred metaphysical interpretation. The gap between the second and third levels is not an embarrassment to be concealed. It is the point at which competing models and new measurements are required.
-
-\subsection{Competing models and the stopping rule}
-
-The motivating problem is not investigated by assuming one total explanation. At minimum, the broader program must compare:
-\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
-\item one foundational observer that generates a contained reality and relinquishes access to its own control;
-\item many genuinely independent observers within a shared physical world;
-\item a physical universe that produces bounded biological observers;
-\item an information-based reality without a singular foundational observer; and
-\item a cognitive model through which an embedded mind organizes severe uncertainty and self-reference.
-\end{enumerate}
-These alternatives may agree on all presently accessible observations. The mathematical theory developed below explains why such agreement matters: an empirical equivalence class does not select one latent representative without added structure.
-
-The research program therefore adopts a stopping rule:
-\begin{quote}
-\emph{Treat the single-observer model, intentional forgetting, revelation, and every external-source model as metaphysical hypotheses unless they produce a unique, testable prediction that competing models do not produce.}
-\end{quote}
-The rule follows Peirce's fallibilist treatment of inquiry and Popper's demand that a scientific proposal expose itself to possible failure rather than acquire protection through reinterpretation \parencite{Peirce1877,Popper1959}. It is also consistent with current efforts to make theories of consciousness explicit, preregistered, and vulnerable to divergent outcomes rather than protected by post hoc reinterpretation \parencite{Cogitate2025,Robinson2025}. It does not abandon the hypotheses. It specifies what would count as progress.
-
-\subsection{The interface problem}
-
-Scientific observation is mediated. A physical system interacts with an apparatus. The apparatus samples selected variables. A reconstruction procedure converts sampled values into an accessible representation. A scientist or biological observer then forms an estimate, report, or decision from that representation. The final statement is separated from the source state by a chain of transformations.
-
-This chain can be reliable without being invertible. An instrument may report every quantity that it was designed to report, and each report may still correspond to more than one source state. The issue is not necessarily measurement error. The issue is whether the map from source states to accessible states preserves every distinction required for reconstruction.
-
-The problem is often described as information loss. That phrase is too broad. The present theory asks: which distinctions are identified by the interface; which quantities remain constant on the resulting equivalence classes; when does a decoder exist; how are interfaces ordered by the distinctions they preserve; and which model properties survive passage to empirical equivalence classes? These questions lead to fibers, quotient spaces, refinement maps, and hidden extensions.
-
-\subsection{The boundary is operational, distributed, and local}
-
-The word \emph{boundary} does not refer only to a wall at a distant edge of the universe. It refers to any stage at which a mapping no longer preserves all distinctions in its source. Such a stage may occur at measurement, absorption, sampling, encoding, compression, scale transition, biological access, reconstruction, language, or report. A scientific chain can therefore contain many local boundaries rather than one final edge.
-
-This operational interpretation separates two statements that must never be confused:
-\[
-\text{not represented}\neq\text{nonexistent},
-\]
-and
-\[
-\text{not represented}\not\Longrightarrow\text{existent}.
-\]
-A missing distinction is underdetermined by the current interface. It is neither eliminated from ontology nor added to ontology by the theorem. Positive existence and nonexistence claims require evidence beyond the absence of representation.
-
-The same distinction separates structural limitation from intentional prohibition. A noninvertible interface creates a mathematical limit relative to that interface. It does not imply that an observer is intentionally prevented from knowing, that no refined interface can be constructed, or that every form of discovery is impossible.
-
-\subsection{Why the bounded problem matters}
-
-The same logical form appears across established technical settings. State observability and system identification ask whether internal states or dynamical descriptions can be recovered from outputs under specified dynamics and inputs \parencite{Kalman1963,HermannKrener1977,Sontag1998,Ljung1999}. Structural identifiability asks whether parameters or model structures are uniquely determined by idealized outputs \parencite{BellmanAstrom1970,Rothenberg1971,WalterPronzato1997,CobelliDiStefano1980,DiStefanoCobelli1980}. Inverse problem theory distinguishes uniquely solvable problems from nonunique or unstable ones \parencite{Hadamard1923,Tikhonov1963,Engl1996}.
-
-Other fields compare accessible behavior rather than internal identity. Blackwell's theory orders statistical experiments by informativeness for decision problems \parencite{Blackwell1951,Blackwell1953,Torgersen1991}. Automata and process semantics compare systems that cannot be distinguished by designated observations \parencite{Moore1956,Park1981,Milner1989,LarsenSkou1991,Rutten2000}. Causal graphical models and latent representation learning likewise require added assumptions to identify hidden structure \parencite{VermaPearl1990,Chickering1995,Andersson1997,Pearl2009,Locatello2019,Hyvarinen2019,Khemakhem2020,Gresele2020,XiBloemReddy2023}.
-
-These traditions are adjacent, not identical. The present framework is deterministic, set based, and static. Its purpose is to isolate the consequence of a specified observation boundary before probability, dynamics, intervention, embodiment, or optimization is added.
-
-\subsection{Mathematical route and contribution}
-
-The paper follows one sequence. An observation map induces an equivalence relation on source states. The quotient retains exactly the distinctions represented by the interface. Quantities available through the interface factor through that quotient. An exact decoder requires injectivity. Interface refinement induces maps between observable quotients. Generative models are compared by reachable observations, and observation-only experiments are invariant within the resulting empirical classes. Hidden extensions then provide explicit countermodels to latent uniqueness.
-
-The contribution is the integrated theorem sequence:
-\[
-\begin{aligned}
-\text{observation map}
-&\Longrightarrow \text{observation fibers and quotient},\\
-\text{fiber constancy}
-&\Longleftrightarrow \text{factorization through the quotient},\\
-\text{exact decoder}
-&\Longrightarrow \text{injective observation},\\
-\text{interface refinement}
-&\Longrightarrow \text{surjective quotient map},\\
-\text{equal reachable sets}
-&\Longrightarrow \text{equal observation-only outcomes},\\
-\text{strict hidden extension}
-&\Longrightarrow \text{same empirical class but no exact decoder}.
-\end{aligned}
-\]
-The empirical model quotient then characterizes exactly which properties and measurements are fixed by the accessible boundary.
-
-\subsection{Scope and organization}
-
-The theorem is mathematical. The simulations are finite illustrations. The broader claims about self-reference, meaning, consciousness, death, light, a foundational observer, intentional forgetting, or external reality are not used as premises. They are retained in later sections as interpretations, application hypotheses, and future research questions with explicit failure conditions.
-
-The next section gives the complete recursive architecture and the taxonomy of transformations. The mathematical sections then develop interfaces, quotients, refinement, models, experiments, profiles, hidden extensions, the empirical quotient, and the synthesis theorem. Computational methods and results follow. The final sections return to interpretation, related work, limitations, hypothesis testing, and the original question.
