-\section{Interpretation and scientific significance}
-\label{sec:significance}
-
-\subsection{The result is a limit on inference, not a positive ontology}
-
-The synthesis theorem is constructive, but the hidden coordinate is a mathematical witness. It demonstrates that reachable-observation equality does not select a unique latent representation from the declared model class. The theorem does not select the extension as physically realized, prove that nature contains a Boolean hidden variable, or establish a single-observer ontology. It establishes a burden of proof for any claim that the accessible boundary uniquely determines latent structure.
-
-That burden can be met in a domain-specific model. Dynamics, interventions, conservation laws, regularity conditions, minimality criteria, priors, known cardinality, or a refined interface may exclude hidden extensions. The theorem does not reject those conditions. It shows that they must be stated because they are not contained in equality of reachable observations alone.
-
-\subsection{Complete accessible information is relative to an interface}
-
-The identity experiment returns every reachable accessible observation without downstream compression. The hidden extension remains empirically equivalent to the base even under that experiment. The limitation is already present in the latent-to-observation map.
-
-The complete predicate profile reaches the same conclusion from another direction. Agreement on every accessible predicate characterizes equality of reachable observation sets. Exact-decoder status can still vary inside the empirical class. The quotient formulation gives the precise result: a property is empirically identifiable exactly when it is constant on empirical-equivalence classes. Exact latent reconstructability is not.
-
-The interpretive safeguard is therefore two-sided:
-\[
-\text{not represented}\neq\text{nonexistent},
-\qquad
-\text{not represented}\not\Longrightarrow\text{existent}.
-\]
-The theorem establishes underdetermination. It does not establish ontology.
-
-\subsection{Paradox as a candidate boundary signal}
-
-The motivating discussion treats paradox not only as a contradiction to be eliminated but as a possible indicator that a representational system has reached the limits of its own closure. The candidate interpretation is:
-\begin{quote}
-\emph{Paradox is not necessarily a defect in the system. It may be a measurement of where the system's representational closure fails.}
-\end{quote}
-This is not a consequence of noninjectivity alone. A contradiction can result from inconsistent premises, ambiguous language, type confusion, numerical error, or a genuinely undecidable or undefinable construction. Classical work on type restrictions, incompleteness, semantic undefinability, and diagonalization shows several distinct mechanisms by which self-reference produces limits \parencite{Russell1908,Godel1931,Tarski1936,Lawvere1969}. Recent work on self-referential living systems likewise argues that self-reference should be modeled rather than merely removed \parencite{Abramsky2025}.
-
-The future scientific task is therefore diagnostic. A proposed boundary signal must distinguish at least four cases: an ordinary modeling error, an unresolved empirical equivalence class, a formal limitation, and a genuine change in representational access. Until such distinctions are operationalized, paradox remains a research cue rather than evidence for a metaphysical conclusion.
-
-\subsection{Containment is not falseness}
-
-A contained or dependent representation can be real at the level on which it operates. A simulated trajectory is real as a trajectory in the simulation. A neural representation is real as a neural state even when it is not identical to its external cause. A social commitment, pain report, mathematical proof, or memory can be relationally real without being an observer-independent primitive.
-
-The corresponding interpretive proposition is:
-\begin{quote}
-\emph{Containment is not the same as falseness. Dependence is not the same as nonexistence.}
-\end{quote}
-This proposition does not decide whether the containing structure is physical, informational, biological, computational, or metaphysical. It rejects only the inference that dependence makes an experience or relation unreal. The distinction is compatible with Kantian, constructivist, cybernetic, and autopoietic traditions that separate the conditions of access from a claim that experience is mere illusion \parencite{Kant1998,VonFoerster1981,MaturanaVarela1980}.
-
-\subsection{Internal meaning and external absoluteness}
-
-Freedom, meaning, beauty, certainty, and truth can function as system-relative relations without thereby being either arbitrary or externally absolute. The mathematical analogue is a quotient invariant: a property can be completely well defined on the accessible quotient even when it does not identify a unique latent representative. Internal validity and latent uniqueness are different questions.
-
-The stronger philosophical hypothesis is that what observers call meaning is produced through recursive interaction among the system, its interface, and its history. In this view, meaning is not diminished by being relational. It is constrained by the transformations under which it remains stable. This hypothesis is related to cybernetic and enactive accounts of sense-making \parencite{MaturanaVarela1980,VonFoerster1981} and to current generative-model approaches in which an agent's world model determines the states it can infer and act upon \parencite{Parr2022,Laukkonen2025}. It is not proved by the quotient theorem.
-
-\subsection{The embedded observer problem}
-
-The present formalization is stated from the external analyst's viewpoint. The broader question concerns an observer whose body, cognition, instruments, language, and inference rules are internal to the system. Such an observer may model itself, act on the world, and thereby change the data from which its next model is constructed.
-
-Active inference explicitly models recurrent perception-action cycles and hidden causes inferred through observations \parencite{Friston2010,Parr2022,Friston2025ScaleFree}. Recent proposals connect recursive world modeling, precision control, and consciousness \parencite{Laukkonen2025,Whyte2026,Robinson2025}. These theories do not establish the candidate invariant, and the present theorem does not establish their consciousness claims. Their relevance is structural: they supply mathematical languages for systems whose internal models participate in the dynamics being modeled.
-
-\subsection{Competing hypotheses and discriminating predictions}
-
-The single-observer hypothesis, a plural-observer physical universe, an information-based ontology without a singular observer, and a cognitive uncertainty model can all be compatible with the same current observations. The appropriate response is not to select the most emotionally powerful explanation. It is to identify a prediction on which the models diverge.
-
-The stopping rule is therefore retained as a scientific constraint:
-\begin{center}
-\fbox{\parbox{0.86\textwidth}{\centering An external or single-observer hypothesis earns support only through a discriminating prediction.}}
-\end{center}
-This principle is illustrated by recent adversarial collaborations in consciousness science, where competing theories were required to state divergent predictions in advance and were tested on common multimodal data \parencite{Cogitate2025,Robinson2025}. Those studies challenge major predictions without delivering a final theory. Their importance here is methodological: empirical equivalence should be broken by prospective tests, not by adding an unmeasured ontology after the result.
-
-
-\subsection{Constraint, self-organization, and the formation of unified regimes}
-
-The hidden-extension theorem is not a theory of emergence, but it clarifies what an emergence theory must add. Prigogine's nonequilibrium thermodynamics, Kauffman's studies of self-organization, and Deacon's constraint-based account of emergence each ask how organized macroscopic regimes arise and persist without being reducible to a list of local components \parencite{NicolisPrigogine1977,Kauffman1993,Deacon2011}. Grossberg develops a neural account in which resonant matching and adaptive dynamics stabilize coherent cognitive states \parencite{Grossberg2021}. Complexity research emphasizes that the relevant organization may reside in interactions, constraints, and multiscale regularities rather than in one component \parencite{Mitchell2009}.
-
-These programs are relevant to the materialism and consciousness application because they supply candidate dynamics for reducing effective degrees of freedom and maintaining a reportable regime. They do not establish that consciousness is identical to low entropy, integration, resonance, or constraint. The interface analysis contributes a prior question: which features of any proposed unified regime are actually identifiable through the declared experimental and biological interfaces?
-
-
-\subsection{Exact formal logic and bounded physical interfaces}
-
-The exactness of a formal proof and the boundedness of a physical interface are compatible statements. Formal derivation can be exact relative to the encoded definitions and rules. The physical process that stores, checks, displays, or interprets that derivation still uses a finite device and a declared encoding. The conclusion is therefore not that ``everything is merely approximate.'' It is that approximation enters when an abstract object is implemented, measured, translated, or applied to a physical domain.
-
-This prevents several category errors. Computational logic is not automatically identical to human reasoning. A brain--computer interface does not expose the complete neural or experiential state of its user. A quantum processor does not present its quantum state directly to a classical observer. An analog device does not solve a differential equation independently of calibration and physical tolerance. A guidance system does not possess an external view of its own trajectory. A sensor suite does not expose the complete state of the object or environment it estimates. A particle detector does not turn an exclusion contour into an observer-independent statement of nonexistence. In every case, the scientific claim must identify the state represented, the map used, the result returned, and the distinctions the interface cannot resolve.
-
-\subsection{Why the distinction matters in scientific practice}
-
-Scientific models often contain unmeasured variables. A fit to observable data can be accurate while the internal explanation remains nonunique. Structural identifiability analysis was developed to detect this problem before parameter estimates are interpreted mechanistically \parencite{BellmanAstrom1970,CobelliDiStefano1980,Raue2009,Chis2011,Villaverde2016,Guillaume2019}. Markov-equivalent causal graphs and nonidentifiable learned representations provide related examples \parencite{VermaPearl1990,Chickering1995,Andersson1997,Locatello2019}.
-
-Interface refinement provides a constructive response. Rather than asking only whether a model is identifiable under the current measurement, one can ask which fibers must be split to identify the property of interest. A refined sensor, additional modality, temporal trajectory, or intervention can replace the current equivalence relation by a finer one.
-
-\subsection{Broad conclusion of the verified layer}
-
-The mathematical conclusion can be stated without metaphysical commitment:
-\[
-\text{complete at the observation boundary}
-\quad\not\Longrightarrow\quad
-\text{complete at the latent level}.
-\]
-The open question begins where this theorem ends: determining whether similar boundary-relative limitations recur in formal self-reference, embedded cognition, biology, artificial intelligence, and physical measurement, and whether any common invariant survives those domain changes.
