-\section{Conclusion}
-\label{sec:conclusion}
-
-The paper began with a question broader than latent reconstruction: can an embedded representational system fully perceive or justify the boundary that generates its own capacity to represent? The present work does not answer that question in general. It proves a bounded result that any adequate answer must respect.
-
-A declared observation map partitions source states into fibers. Quantities available only through the observation are constant on those fibers. Internal tests receive the same input from observationally equivalent states. A globally exact decoder requires injectivity. The observable quotient retains exactly the distinctions represented by the interface, while refinement orders interfaces by the distinctions they preserve.
-
-At the model level, equality of reachable observation sets defines empirical equivalence. Empirically equivalent models have the same possible outcomes under every observation-only experiment and agree on complete accessible predicate profiles. Hidden extensions show what that completeness omits. Latent multiplicity, fiber size, and exact-decoder status can change while the complete reachable observation set remains fixed.
-
-The synthesis theorem combines these facts. Every inhabited model and admissible experiment admit a strict latent cover in the same empirical class, with the same reachable observations and possible outcomes, but without an exact latent decoder. The finite simulation suite displays the construction in enumerated models and reproduces the interface-refinement maps and empirical classes used in the theorem's interpretation.
-
-The exact conclusion is:
-\[
-\text{complete access to an observation boundary}
-\not\Longrightarrow
-\text{complete access to latent structure}.
-\]
-The corresponding safeguard is equally important:
-\[
-\text{not represented}\neq\text{nonexistent},
-\qquad
-\text{not represented}\not\Longrightarrow\text{existent}.
-\]
-The theorem establishes underdetermination rather than ontology.
-
-Paradox may be investigated as a possible boundary signal. Embedded self-modeling requires a theory in which the observer and its interface participate in the same dynamics. Containment does not automatically imply falseness, and relational meaning can remain real within a containing system. Materialism, consciousness, death, light, a foundational observer, intentional forgetting, and external-source proposals remain hypotheses. None inherits proof from the synthesis theorem. Each must define its observables, competing models, and failure conditions.
-
-
-Leibnizian perspective explains why one world may admit different internally complete views; Peircean and Popperian inquiry explain why those views must be exposed to consequences that could separate them; Whiteheadian process and cybernetic feedback explain why the observer route is dynamic rather than a passive snapshot \parencite{Leibniz1989,Peirce1877,Peirce1878,Popper1959,Whitehead1978,Wiener1948}. These traditions do not prove the theorem, and the theorem does not establish their metaphysical conclusions. It gives a precise condition they must respect whenever a proposed observer map is noninjective.
-
-
-The same principle applies across heterogeneous physical interfaces. Digital, analog, quantum, neural, and human systems can implement different representations even when they return a common abstract result. A sensor suite combines partial measurements into a state estimate without exposing the complete state it represents. An inertial guidance system acts from that estimate and its error model rather than an external view of its actual path. Particle physics distinguishes theoretical prohibition, detector inaccessibility, and statistical exclusion. These examples are not interchangeable, but each shows why the meaning of an output depends on the interface that made it accessible.
-
-The methodological rule is final: a preferred ontology earns support only when it predicts an accessible difference that empirically equivalent alternatives do not share. Where no such difference is available, the honest result is an equivalence class, not a verdict.
-
-The motivating statement is: \emph{life is about living for the sake of the paradoxes that rule us}. In scientific form, the task is to determine which paradoxes are errors, which are unresolved alternatives, which are formal limits, and which reveal a boundary that a refined interface can make newly visible.
